    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`AcceptanceTest` passes, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `customerCode` format is now `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` with a per-city sequence. Three touch points, each kept small:\n\n1. **`CityCodes.java`** (new) \u2014 a small, cohesive helper mirroring the existing `Namesakes`/`Households` pattern. `count(existing, candidate)` returns how many owners already share the candidate's city (case-insensitive). No surrounding complexity to reason about.\n\n2. **`Owner.assignCustomerCode`** \u2014 one method, CC 1, now prepends the upper-cased first three letters of the city:\n   ```java\n   this.customerCode = getCity().substring(0, 3).toUpperCase() + \"-\"\n       + getLastName().substring(0, 3).toUpperCase() + String.format(\"-%04d\", sequence);\n   ```\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 a single-line argument swap, from the global `owners.size() + 1` to the per-city `CityCodes.count(owners, owner) + 1`. The method's complexity is unchanged.\n\nEverything derived from `customerCode` \u2014 notably `membershipNumber` in `OwnerMapper` (`customerCode + \"-M\" + <year>`) \u2014 rebuilds automatically, since it reads `getCustomerCode()` at mapping time rather than storing a copy. So no further edits were needed for derived values.\n\nThe new-behaviour logic lives almost entirely in the new isolated helper, so no existing large method or class grew.",
